WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
This map can’t see your house.
An illustrative regional cell: approximately 110 km.

00:02.000 --> 00:05.000
But floods happen street by street.
Roads. Homes. People.

00:05.000 --> 00:08.000
From regional probability to a research target
of approximately 10 metre flood-depth resolution.

00:08.000 --> 00:11.000
East Fork Pigeon River, western North Carolina.
Near Canton, Haywood County.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.000
Tropical Storm Fred. August 17, 2021.
19,800 cfs peak flow. 16.03 ft gauge stage.
Gauge stage is not building water depth.

00:15.000 --> 00:19.000
NeuralHydrology → river flow → river height → HAND → flood depth.
Method concept. Calibration is required.

00:19.000 --> 00:22.000
Compare predictions with real floods.
Observed gauge data is available; calibrated depth remains a research target.

00:22.000 --> 00:24.500
Depth is only half the story.

00:24.500 --> 00:27.000
Who was in the water?
Connect flood risk and community data.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
Hurricane Helene. September 27, 2024.
15,900 cfs peak flow. 14.10 ft gauge stage.

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
Aquatic Sustainability.
See the water. See who it reaches.
Illustrative 3D scenes. Research demonstration.
